---
// The explanation below the Lab: the text, the symbols, how the network
// reads, memory, temperature, how it learned, and the model in short.
// Static HTML; formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import type { Dataset } from './data';

import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

interface Props {
  dataset: Dataset;
}

const { dataset } = Astro.props;
const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
const { subjects, verbs, objects } = dataset.words;
const sentence = `${subjects[0] ?? ''} ${verbs[0] ?? ''} ${objects[0] ?? ''}.`;
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-data">
    <h2 id="rnn-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      A small neural network has read many short, made-up sentences, one
      character at a time, and learned to guess the next character. Each
      sentence is someone, doing something, somewhere: “{sentence}”
    </p>
    <p>
      Each column is one step: the character the network has just read, and
      under it the network's memory after reading it, {dataset.hidden} numbers
      between −1 and 1. The bars show its guesses for the next character after
      the selected step.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-symbols">
    <h2 id="rnn-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>xₜ</dt>
        <dd>The character read at step t.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>hₜ</dt>
        <dd>The memory (hidden state) after step t.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Wₓ, Wₕ, W_y</dt>
        <dd>
          The weights the network learned: how a character, the old memory and
          the memory feed into the next step.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>zₜ</dt>
        <dd>One score for every possible next character.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">T</dt>
        <dd>The temperature: how bold or cautious the guesses are.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-how">
    <h2 id="rnn-how">How the network reads</h2>
    <p>
      At every step, the network takes the new character and its memory of
      everything before, mixes them with its weights, and squeezes the result
      between −1 and 1. That is its new memory. From the memory, it scores every
      possible next character.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.step} />
    <p>
      The same weights are used at every step: the network is one small machine
      run over and over, passing its memory along. That loop is what makes it
      recurrent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-memory">
    <h2 id="rnn-memory">Memory</h2>
    <p>
      After “the c”, the network guesses “a”, because “cat” is the only word
      here starting with c after “the”. To know that, it must remember the “the”
      it read a few steps ago. Watch the memory strips: some numbers flip when a
      word ends, others hold steady across a word. Nobody told the network what
      to remember; it found out while learning.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its memory is short, though. A plain network like this one forgets
      quickly, so it cannot keep track of a whole page. Bigger models add gates
      that decide what to keep and what to forget.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-temperature">
    <h2 id="rnn-temperature">Temperature, and surprise</h2>
    <p>
      The scores become chances with softmax. Dividing the scores by the
      temperature T first changes how bold the network is: a low T makes the top
      guess even more likely, a high T spreads the chances out. Press{' '}
      <strong>Write</strong> at different temperatures: low ones repeat safe
      words, high ones invent strange ones.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.softmax} />
    <p>
      Surprise measures how well the network predicted what really came next.
      Averaged over the text, it is lower the better the network knows this kind
      of sentence. Type something unusual and watch it rise.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.surprise} />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-learn">
    <h2 id="rnn-learn">How it learned</h2>
    <p>
      The weights started as small random numbers. The network read the training
      sentences, and after every 40 characters it measured its total surprise
      and nudged every weight a little to lower it, working back through the
      steps (backpropagation through time). After a few thousand rounds, it
      guesses most characters well. The sentences used these words:{' '}
      {subjects.join(', ')}; {verbs.join(', ')}; {objects.join(', ')}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rnn-summary">
    <h2 id="rnn-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Read</strong> one character at a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remember:</strong> mix the character into the memory, using the
        same weights at every step.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Guess</strong> the next character from the memory, as chances.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Learn</strong> by nudging the weights to make the real next
        character less surprising.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
